      "tests": [
        {
          "test_id": "DRC_SUBLETTING_C01",
          "status": "UNRESOLVED",
          "support_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L45:span1",
              "quote": "The appellant alleged that the tenant sub-let the premises without her consent in writingwhich, was a requirement of law, as the sub-tenancy was created after 9/06/1952."
            }
          ],
          "opposition_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L46:span3",
              "quote": "Two of the sub-tenants, namely, Shri Kirori Mal and Shri Dewan Chand Gupta were in occupation prior to the creation to tenancy in favour of Onkar Nath by the then owner/landlord Shri Inder Narain."
            },
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L48:span5",
              "quote": "two subtenants who were admittedly in possession on the date of creation of tenancy and the same was with the consent of the landlord."
            }
          ],
          "gap_reason": "The only supplied statement placing the alleged sub-tenancy after 9 June 1952 is the appellant's allegation. The supplied record does not give the date on which Onkar Nath's tenancy was created or a finding fixing the date of any later disposition by him. The contrary record that the two named occupants were already in possession before, and at the creation of, Onkar Nath's tenancy makes the timing of a tenant-created disposition materially contested.",
          "other_combinations": [
            "Kirori Mal and Dewan Chand Gupta should each be tested separately because the supplied record does not establish that any later disposition concerning one necessarily concerned the other.",
            "A later change in legal rights after Onkar Nath's tenancy began could differ from the pre-existing physical occupation, but no such later transaction or date is supplied."
          ],
          "reason": "The post-9-June-1952 proposition is not established by a finding. It is asserted by the appellant, while the supplied record contains contrary timing facts showing that the two named occupants pre-dated Onkar Nath's tenancy. Without the tenancy-creation date and without evidence of a later qualifying transaction by Onkar Nath, the literal temporal test remains unresolved."
        },
        {
          "test_id": "DRC_SUBLETTING_C02",
          "status": "UNRESOLVED",
          "support_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L45:span1",
              "quote": "The appellant alleged that the tenant sub-let the premises without her consent in writingwhich, was a requirement of law, as the sub-tenancy was created after 9/06/1952."
            },
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L48:span5",
              "quote": "two subtenants who were admittedly in possession on the date of creation of tenancy and the same was with the consent of the landlord."
            }
          ],
          "opposition_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L46:span3",
              "quote": "Two of the sub-tenants, namely, Shri Kirori Mal and Shri Dewan Chand Gupta were in occupation prior to the creation to tenancy in favour of Onkar Nath by the then owner/landlord Shri Inder Narain."
            },
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L48:span5",
              "quote": "two subtenants who were admittedly in possession on the date of creation of tenancy and the same was with the consent of the landlord."
            }
          ],
          "gap_reason": "The record uses the label 'sub-tenants' and contains an allegation of subletting, but it does not supply facts establishing the specific legal incidents required by the test: a landlord-and-tenant relationship between Onkar Nath and each purported subtenant, transfer of an interest in the estate, payment of rent, and a right to possession as against Onkar Nath. The pre-existing occupation with landlord consent also creates a material alternative explanation.",
          "other_combinations": [
            "Kirori Mal could have a different legal arrangement from Dewan Chand Gupta; the supplied excerpts do not establish identical terms.",
            "The two occupants could have held rights derived from the then owner/landlord rather than from Onkar Nath; the supplied excerpts do not resolve that possibility.",
            "The referenced rent note might bear on the relationships, but only the respondent's description that it mentions the pre-existing occupation is supplied; its operative terms are not."
          ],
          "reason": "A bare allegation and the descriptive label 'sub-tenants' do not establish all incidents that the supplied legal test requires. At the same time, the record affirmatively states that the two named occupants were present before Onkar Nath's tenancy and that their possession at the tenancy's creation was with the landlord's consent. The source material therefore leaves the legal relationship and required tenancy incidents unresolved."
        },
        {
          "test_id": "DRC_SUBLETTING_C04",
          "status": "UNRESOLVED",
          "support_refs": [],
          "opposition_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L46:span3",
              "quote": "Two of the sub-tenants, namely, Shri Kirori Mal and Shri Dewan Chand Gupta were in occupation prior to the creation to tenancy in favour of Onkar Nath by the then owner/landlord Shri Inder Narain."
            },
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L48:span5",
              "quote": "two subtenants who were admittedly in possession on the date of creation of tenancy and the same was with the consent of the landlord."
            }
          ],
          "gap_reason": "The supplied record does not establish that Onkar Nath vested possession in either occupant by divesting himself of both physical possession and the right to possession. Pre-existing occupation is contrary to a simple theory that Onkar Nath first placed them in physical possession, but it does not rule out a later change in legal possessory rights. No such later change is supplied.",
          "other_combinations": [
            "The possession of Kirori Mal and Dewan Chand Gupta must be assessed separately because the record does not establish the same legal rights for both.",
            "A theory based on later divestment of legal possession, despite pre-existing physical occupation, remains logically possible but is not evidenced in the supplied record."
          ],
          "reason": "The tenant-caused divestment required by the parting-with-possession test is neither found nor described. The prior occupation evidence cuts against tenant-originated physical transfer, while the record is silent on whether Onkar Nath later abandoned or transferred his right to possession. The literal test therefore remains unresolved."
        },
        {
          "test_id": "DRC_SUBLETTING_C05",
          "status": "UNRESOLVED",
          "support_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L45:span1",
              "quote": "The appellant alleged that the tenant sub-let the premises without her consent in writingwhich, was a requirement of law, as the sub-tenancy was created after 9/06/1952."
            }
          ],
          "opposition_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L48:span5",
              "quote": "two subtenants who were admittedly in possession on the date of creation of tenancy and the same was with the consent of the landlord."
            },
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L46:span3",
              "quote": "Two of the sub-tenants, namely, Shri Kirori Mal and Shri Dewan Chand Gupta were in occupation prior to the creation to tenancy in favour of Onkar Nath by the then owner/landlord Shri Inder Narain."
            }
          ],
          "gap_reason": "The appellant alleges lack of her written consent, but the supplied prior-court-level statement says the two occupants' possession at the creation of the tenancy was with the landlord's consent. The form of that consent is not stated, the exact landlord referred to in span5 is not expressly named there, and no supplied source establishes whether the consent related to the same later disposition alleged by the appellant.",
          "other_combinations": [
            "Consent by the then owner/landlord to pre-existing occupation is not automatically the same thing as written consent to a later subletting, assignment, or parting with possession by Onkar Nath.",
            "The supplied materials do not establish whether any consent was written, oral, contained in the referenced rent note, or directed to a specific later disposition.",
            "The name 'Inder Nath' in the respondent's description of the rent note is not reconciled by the supplied materials with 'Shri Inder Narain,' the named then owner/landlord."
          ],
          "reason": "The no-written-consent proposition cannot be treated as established merely from the appellant's allegation. The contrary record establishes some landlord consent to the occupants' possession at tenancy creation, but it does not state whether that consent was written or whether it covered the same legally relevant disposition. The required written-consent issue is therefore unresolved."
        },
        {
          "test_id": "DRC_SUBLETTING_C06",
          "status": "UNRESOLVED",
          "support_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L46:span3",
              "quote": "Two of the sub-tenants, namely, Shri Kirori Mal and Shri Dewan Chand Gupta were in occupation prior to the creation to tenancy in favour of Onkar Nath by the then owner/landlord Shri Inder Narain."
            },
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L48:span5",
              "quote": "two subtenants who were admittedly in possession on the date of creation of tenancy and the same was with the consent of the landlord."
            }
          ],
          "opposition_refs": [],
          "gap_reason": "The co-existence of Onkar Nath's tenancy with pre-existing, landlord-consented occupants is compatible with retained legal possession or concurrent user, but the supplied excerpts do not state that Onkar Nath retained legal possession of the same part, had a right to concurrent use, or was not wholly ousted from that part.",
          "other_combinations": [
            "Retained legal possession may differ as between the area occupied by Kirori Mal and the area occupied by Dewan Chand Gupta; no part-by-part facts are supplied.",
            "The referenced rent note could potentially define concurrent rights, but its terms are not supplied beyond the respondent's assertion that it mentions the prior occupation."
          ],
          "reason": "The pre-existing, landlord-consented occupation provides some factual support for a non-exclusive arrangement, but the precise legal-possession condition in this negative test is not established. Because retained legal possession or concurrent use is decisive to defeating the parting-with-possession route, the test remains unresolved."
        },
        {
          "test_id": "DRC_SUBLETTING_C07",
          "status": "SUPPORTED",
          "support_refs": [
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L45:span1",
              "quote": "The appellant alleged that the tenant sub-let the premises without her consent in writingwhich, was a requirement of law, as the sub-tenancy was created after 9/06/1952."
            },
            {
              "source_id": "IK-489898:L45:span2",
              "quote": "The respondent took the plea that sub-tenants already occupying the premises as of right."
            }
          ],
          "opposition_refs": [],
          "gap_reason": null,
          "other_combinations": [
            "The two named occupants could have occupied different parts of the premises; the supplied record does not delineate those parts, but C07 requires only that the challenged disposition concern the whole or any part of the premises.",
            "Support for C07 concerns the scope of the arrangement as alleged. It does not establish that a legally qualifying subletting, assignment, or parting with possession actually occurred; that distinct question belongs to the disposition tests."
          ],
          "reason": "DRC_SUBLETTING_C07 is a premises-scope test, not a test of whether the alleged disposition actually occurred. The appellant's allegation expressly states that the tenant 'sub-let the premises,' and the respondent's plea likewise places the alleged sub-tenants in 'the premises.' Those sources support that the challenged arrangement, as alleged, concerns the premises within the whole-or-any-part formulation. Treating C07 as UNRESOLVED solely because the underlying disposition is not established conflates scope with occurrence."
        }
      ],
